ntdll: system calls write into the emulator's write trap, as the Windows kernel does.

FEX makes a read-write-execute page that holds translated code read-execute, so that a write into it faults into
its tracking of self-modifying code. A write by the program itself reaches FEX that way, but a system call that
writes into such a page for the program faulted on the Unix side, in virtual_handle_fault, and returned
STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION. Windows has no such trap, and the same call succeeds there. FEX's own
BTCpu64NotifyReadFile names the case ("FEX could have write-protected the memory region when a JIT executed the
code, and once the NtReadFile tries returning data, it faults. This requires coordination between Wine and FEX").

Football Manager 27 (Game Pass) stopped at "Loading game database": its copy protection asked for the SMBIOS table
with NtQuerySystemInformation(SystemFirmwareTableInformation) into a buffer on a page next to code it had run,
the call returned STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION after a fault at the buffer's TableBufferLength, and the protection then
read an address it had computed from the missing answer (an unhandled access violation in its own code).
tests/x64-fidelity/syscallwrite: ReadProcessMemory, ReadFile and the firmware table into such a page fail with
ERROR_NOACCESS (998) on arm64-kk-wine11.18-r5 and succeed under Rosetta 2 (M5 Pro, macOS 27.2, 2026-11-11).

- ntdll's ARM64EC side marks the pages the emulator traps: after a successful NtProtectVirtualMemory on the
  current process that leaves a page not writable while GuestProtection (0043) says the program made it
  writable, it passes the range to the Unix side through a private NtSetInformationVirtualMemory class.
- The Unix side keeps the mark in a free page flag (VPROT_HB_EMU_TRAP). Any other protection change clears it,
  and region queries ignore it, so a program sees the same regions as before.
- A write fault from inside a system call (is_inside_syscall) into a marked page makes the host page writable,
  remembers it, and sets a flag in the syscall frame; the dispatcher calls hb_syscall_retrap on the return,
  which gives the page its trap back. The program's own writes take FEX's path as before, and a rewrite of the
  code after such a call still takes effect without a flush (syscallwrite rewrite.no-flush).

A system call that writes over code that already ran leaves FEX's translations of it in place, except NtReadFile,
whose BTCpu64NotifyReadFile already invalidates the buffer after the read.

